WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.328 --> 00:00:05.024
- Failure's not a bad thing.

00:00:05.024 --> 00:00:06.458
- Failure is the mother of all success.

00:00:06.458 --> 00:00:08.013
- You should seek failures.

00:00:08.013 --> 00:00:09.526
- No consideration of failure.

00:00:09.526 --> 00:00:11.936
- You have to be able to
rise from the failure.

00:00:11.936 --> 00:00:12.769
- Failure.

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:13.602
- Definition of failure.

00:00:13.602 --> 00:00:14.850
- Getting things wrong is as important

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:15.963
as getting things right.

00:00:15.963 --> 00:00:17.675
- Failure is on a success train.

00:00:17.675 --> 00:00:18.883
- Hello Believe Nation.

00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:22.642
Today we're going to talk about
how you can defeat failure.

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:24.815
And as always, guys, as
you're watching the videos,

00:00:24.815 --> 00:00:26.981
if you see something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:26.981 --> 00:00:28.781
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:28.781 --> 00:00:31.237
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:31.237 --> 00:00:33.402
And if you write it down
it's much more likely

00:00:33.402 --> 00:00:36.069
to stick with yourself, as well.

00:00:47.511 --> 00:00:49.449
- Yeah, I think failure's
the highway to success,

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.161
and I know that sounds like a platitude--

00:00:51.161 --> 00:00:51.994
- [Woman] It is.

00:00:51.994 --> 00:00:53.284
But society, where people teach us, right?

00:00:53.284 --> 00:00:54.117
- [Woman] Um-hmm.

00:00:54.117 --> 00:00:56.505
- They say, "Oh, if you
fail and feel uncomfortable,

00:00:56.505 --> 00:00:57.765
you've done something wrong."

00:00:57.765 --> 00:00:59.798
And what I try to do in all of my books,

00:00:59.798 --> 00:01:02.267
and my presentations, and my tweets is,

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.017
you cannot get to the mountaintop

00:01:06.248 --> 00:01:09.297
without taking a few missteps.

00:01:09.297 --> 00:01:11.870
And so, a business that
wants to win, you have

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:13.418
to out-fail your competition.

00:01:13.418 --> 00:01:17.285
And as human beings, you know,
failure's not a bad thing.

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:18.992
You know, ask yourself,
"What's the opportunity

00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:20.010
from the failure?"

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:22.179
And get into the game, because
when you get to the last hour

00:01:22.179 --> 00:01:24.697
of your last day, it will not
be the failures you regret,

00:01:24.697 --> 00:01:26.387
it will be all the risks you didn't take.

00:01:26.387 --> 00:01:28.742
- Eventually joined Harper's Bazaar U.S.,

00:01:28.742 --> 00:01:31.742
and in 1976, it was, you were fired.

00:01:33.011 --> 00:01:34.760
And I find that extraordinary.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.071
No, they were very clear
in that they thought

00:01:38.071 --> 00:01:41.158
that I would never undertstand
the American market.

00:01:41.158 --> 00:01:45.107
I remember I was the editor on one shoot

00:01:45.107 --> 00:01:49.367
that was, I think it was
French fashion shows,

00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:52.820
and I decided that it would be interesting

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.994
to have dreadlocks on the models,

00:01:55.994 --> 00:01:58.540
and I think that that pushed them further

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.394
than they could bear.

00:02:00.394 --> 00:02:03.394
So that was it for me, but you know,

00:02:05.085 --> 00:02:09.252
I think it's always part of
life to have some knocks,

00:02:10.751 --> 00:02:12.121
as well as some successes--

00:02:12.121 --> 00:02:13.135
- And to take risks.

00:02:13.135 --> 00:02:17.307
- And, you know, to learn,
so I think it's healthy

00:02:17.307 --> 00:02:19.809
for everyone to lose a job
as well as to gain a job.

00:02:19.809 --> 00:02:23.091
- I came upon a slogan, and I tell it

00:02:23.091 --> 00:02:26.549
to my kids all the time,
I say, "There's no losing,

00:02:26.549 --> 00:02:31.344
only learning, there's no
failure, only opportunities,

00:02:31.344 --> 00:02:34.400
and there's no problems, only solutions."

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.388
So to me what failure
is, failure is the mother

00:02:36.388 --> 00:02:37.421
of all success.

00:02:37.421 --> 00:02:39.251
If it wasn't for Michael
Jordan getting cut

00:02:39.251 --> 00:02:40.441
From his 9th grade basketball team,

00:02:40.441 --> 00:02:43.022
he wouldn't have became Michael Jordan.

00:02:43.022 --> 00:02:46.090
You know, if it wasn't for, I
seen an article the other day,

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.629
where they were talking
about Oprah Winfrey,

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:48.462
and how she got fired,

00:02:48.462 --> 00:02:51.045
because she wasn't good for television.

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:53.527
You know, you got people like Walt Disney,

00:02:53.527 --> 00:02:55.882
who got fired and comments

00:02:55.882 --> 00:02:59.042
from a newspaper saying
he had no imagination.

00:02:59.042 --> 00:03:02.209
(in foreign language)

00:03:03.227 --> 00:03:04.450
These are the same people
that I've got to deal

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.783
with telling me what's not a hit record.

00:03:08.672 --> 00:03:09.505
So--

00:03:09.505 --> 00:03:10.338
- [Man] So what do you tell them,

00:03:10.338 --> 00:03:11.171
do you tell them, you know--

00:03:11.171 --> 00:03:13.194
- You know that all they can do is learn,

00:03:13.194 --> 00:03:14.874
and come back bigger, better, stronger.

00:03:14.874 --> 00:03:16.988
Cause all it's going to do is
lead you in the right direction.

00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:18.752
See, if you're always winning,

00:03:18.752 --> 00:03:21.309
then you don't really
understand what it is to win.

00:03:21.309 --> 00:03:24.323
You got to take those losses,
you got to take those hits.

00:03:24.323 --> 00:03:26.945
There's got to be the values
the peaks, the ups, the downs,

00:03:26.945 --> 00:03:29.665
in order for you to, when it does happen,

00:03:29.665 --> 00:03:31.939
you go, "Wow, (in foreign language)."

00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:33.330
You know, this is what's it's all about.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.673
And not only that,

00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:36.011
it's never about making it, guys,

00:03:36.011 --> 00:03:38.490
it's always about maintaining it.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.100
That's the toughest part.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.827
- You should seek failures.

00:03:42.827 --> 00:03:45.964
See, if you're playing it
safe, you're not going to win.

00:03:45.964 --> 00:03:50.586
If you're playing it safe,
like that old saying, you know,

00:03:50.586 --> 00:03:52.198
you were taught when
you were a little girl,

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:53.366
"Better to be safe than sorry."

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:54.789
Well, that's a bunch of crap.

00:03:54.789 --> 00:03:57.956
It's not better to be safe than sorry.

00:03:58.864 --> 00:04:00.407
It's by trying things

00:04:00.407 --> 00:04:03.088
that you figure out how far you can go.

00:04:03.088 --> 00:04:06.304
You've got to get outside the box.

00:04:06.304 --> 00:04:09.988
Like it's reported,
Edison tried 3,000 ways

00:04:09.988 --> 00:04:11.795
before he built the incandescent light.

00:04:11.795 --> 00:04:14.598
He said, he didn't fail 3,000 times,

00:04:14.598 --> 00:04:17.081
he said there was 3,000 different steps

00:04:17.081 --> 00:04:19.893
to building a light bulb.

00:04:19.893 --> 00:04:22.866
Well, there's different steps
to get where you're going.

00:04:22.866 --> 00:04:25.023
And I really do treat winning

00:04:25.023 --> 00:04:26.645
and losing exactly the same.

00:04:26.645 --> 00:04:29.163
I do not let it upset me.

00:04:29.163 --> 00:04:30.534
- Do you think that's a big part

00:04:30.534 --> 00:04:33.136
of why you're so successful?

00:04:33.136 --> 00:04:36.432
- Well, it's part of why I enjoy it.

00:04:36.432 --> 00:04:39.604
Yeah, I think it is,
cause I'll try anything.

00:04:39.604 --> 00:04:42.415
Like, if I'm into an idea,

00:04:42.415 --> 00:04:46.118
the idea of losing never enters my mind.

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:48.701
See, I have worked with giants.

00:04:49.535 --> 00:04:54.420
I had the good fortune of
working with Sir Edmund Hillary.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.876
He was the first man to
stand on top of the world.

00:04:57.876 --> 00:05:02.043
And I worked with him on
three different occasions.

00:05:03.238 --> 00:05:06.081
And the only difference in
Ed Hillary and Bob Proctor,

00:05:06.081 --> 00:05:07.702
is his size.

00:05:07.702 --> 00:05:10.595
Now he's gone now, but he was a big man.

00:05:10.595 --> 00:05:11.799
When he shook hands with me,

00:05:11.799 --> 00:05:13.423
his hand wrapped right around mine.

00:05:13.423 --> 00:05:15.923
I felt like a dwarf, you know.

00:05:17.121 --> 00:05:20.495
And, in talking to him, like
it never entered his mind

00:05:20.495 --> 00:05:22.403
he wouldn't get to the
top of the mountain,

00:05:22.403 --> 00:05:25.820
but he failed in 1951, he failed in 1952,

00:05:27.084 --> 00:05:29.600
and it wasn't until 1953
that he got to the top

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:30.704
of the mountain.

00:05:30.704 --> 00:05:34.244
You know, people had died for
centuries trying to do that.

00:05:34.244 --> 00:05:38.391
But he said I knew if
I could see it in here,

00:05:38.391 --> 00:05:39.858
I could hold it out here.

00:05:39.858 --> 00:05:42.771
Stella Mann wrote that in
a book, "If you can see it

00:05:42.771 --> 00:05:44.681
in your head, you can
hold it in your hand."

00:05:44.681 --> 00:05:47.895
And that's right, and if
you're not prepared to lose,

00:05:47.895 --> 00:05:49.356
you're never going to win,

00:05:49.356 --> 00:05:51.640
because you're always playing it safe,

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.204
and there's no reward in that.

00:05:54.204 --> 00:05:58.398
- I've always had this really
gross, dangerous mentality

00:05:58.398 --> 00:06:00.981
of no consideration of failure.

00:06:03.524 --> 00:06:05.561
Just never even considering
the thought of failing.

00:06:05.561 --> 00:06:09.728
Like if I want something,
I just go until I get it.

00:06:11.701 --> 00:06:14.701
And that doesn't always work, but...

00:06:16.087 --> 00:06:16.920
- I think, like you said,

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.041
every single artist goes through that,

00:06:19.041 --> 00:06:21.155
and I think a lot of times you think

00:06:21.155 --> 00:06:23.551
that the great writers are the people,

00:06:23.551 --> 00:06:24.788
they just write hit, after hit, after hit.

00:06:24.788 --> 00:06:25.871
And they do, but it's also

00:06:25.871 --> 00:06:29.380
because they get through all
the terrible songs first,

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:33.491
and I think that's kind of
every single artist's story.

00:06:33.491 --> 00:06:36.128
I mean, I love to tell, when people ask,

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:38.095
"What's you best advice for,
you know, upcoming musician,

00:06:38.095 --> 00:06:41.878
or anybody," I think it's that
you have to be able to rise

00:06:41.878 --> 00:06:44.765
from the failures, is what
makes a successful person.

00:06:44.765 --> 00:06:47.119
Because everybody's going to have mishaps.

00:06:47.119 --> 00:06:49.624
They're going to fall down,
and it's, you know, the people

00:06:49.624 --> 00:06:51.208
that continuously pick themselves up

00:06:51.208 --> 00:06:53.719
that are the ones that are
going to find their voice,

00:06:53.719 --> 00:06:55.485
make it, whatever you want to call it.

00:06:55.485 --> 00:06:56.921
- I mean, I'm pretty sure
there are people who,

00:06:56.921 --> 00:06:59.171
you know, had their doubts.

00:07:02.337 --> 00:07:04.210
As I say, you know, art is subjective.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:07.011
Some people have an
opinion they like about it,

00:07:07.011 --> 00:07:08.311
and of course, they have,
they're entitled to,

00:07:08.311 --> 00:07:10.671
because they're paying their money for it.

00:07:10.671 --> 00:07:14.003
But, you know, I never let that stop me.

00:07:14.003 --> 00:07:16.687
I've always been able to
turn my head into great,

00:07:16.687 --> 00:07:21.121
and not really accept any
down moments, low peaks,

00:07:21.121 --> 00:07:24.415
as a failure, but yet, a
lesson, to teach me how

00:07:24.415 --> 00:07:28.498
to be better, stronger,
more wise, and resilient.

00:07:30.744 --> 00:07:34.038
You know, I really have never
gotten to the negative stuff.

00:07:34.038 --> 00:07:36.238
I just, I don't really absorb it.

00:07:36.238 --> 00:07:37.234
- As an entrepreneur,

00:07:37.234 --> 00:07:38.861
there actually is a definition of failure.

00:07:38.861 --> 00:07:40.567
And a lot of people ask
me, "When you move on,"

00:07:40.567 --> 00:07:43.084
when do you stop what you're doing,

00:07:43.084 --> 00:07:46.377
and say, "Maybe this isn't working out?"

00:07:46.377 --> 00:07:49.349
And I like to say, "Well,
first you have to believe in,

00:07:49.349 --> 00:07:52.564
if you keep believing in what
you're doing, keep going."

00:07:52.564 --> 00:07:54.067
But there's one other criteria.

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:56.965
If keeping going means you are going

00:07:56.965 --> 00:08:01.353
to do severe physical or
mental damage to yourself,

00:08:01.353 --> 00:08:03.305
then you should move on.

00:08:03.305 --> 00:08:04.138
- [Man] Yeah.

00:08:04.138 --> 00:08:07.518
- And my last company, I
sort of got to that point.

00:08:07.518 --> 00:08:10.695
It was a success for me, I
mean, I ultimately sold it

00:08:10.695 --> 00:08:14.612
to a large company out
of Boston called Akamai,

00:08:15.876 --> 00:08:20.420
but I was at that point
where I had gone seven years,

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.242
and it was really hard times,

00:08:22.242 --> 00:08:24.523
and I knew that if I had kept going

00:08:24.523 --> 00:08:27.815
in this little blip of
success we started to see,

00:08:27.815 --> 00:08:30.485
if that was a mirage, I
knew I would kind of go,

00:08:30.485 --> 00:08:34.060
I would start to go crazy, like for real.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:37.351
Failure is when, is maybe when,

00:08:37.351 --> 00:08:38.318
at least I feel like I failed

00:08:38.318 --> 00:08:41.862
when I didn't put everything
I had into a problem.

00:08:41.862 --> 00:08:43.079
So really, it goes back

00:08:43.079 --> 00:08:45.355
to the analogy I made at the beginning.

00:08:45.355 --> 00:08:47.256
If you don't put it all out there,

00:08:47.256 --> 00:08:50.102
if you don't put everything
you got into solving a problem,

00:08:50.102 --> 00:08:51.531
then that's failure.

00:08:51.531 --> 00:08:53.681
- Overall, that very thumbnail sketch

00:08:53.681 --> 00:08:58.139
of a very interesting and
a very inspiring life,

00:08:58.139 --> 00:09:01.009
what do you think you've learned most,

00:09:01.009 --> 00:09:04.426
what would you do
differently, in other words.

00:09:04.426 --> 00:09:06.629
- Do you know, I wouldn't
do anything differently,

00:09:06.629 --> 00:09:08.459
because I think getting things wrong,

00:09:08.459 --> 00:09:11.876
as we've just talked
about, is as important

00:09:11.876 --> 00:09:13.015
as getting things right.

00:09:13.015 --> 00:09:15.007
So I wouldn't change
anything because actually,

00:09:15.007 --> 00:09:17.324
I have learned a lot.

00:09:17.324 --> 00:09:19.965
And the thing that I've
learned is, I know what I value

00:09:19.965 --> 00:09:21.547
in life, I know what's important to me,

00:09:21.547 --> 00:09:23.461
I know what I want to spend my time doing,

00:09:23.461 --> 00:09:25.414
I know the differences
that I want to make,

00:09:25.414 --> 00:09:26.415
and more important,

00:09:26.415 --> 00:09:29.100
I absolutely know what I don't want to do.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:29.933
- I'm not going to do
what everybody else does,

00:09:29.933 --> 00:09:32.087
I'm just going to do what I want to do,

00:09:32.087 --> 00:09:34.740
because life's too short,
and I can't live up

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.028
to other people's expectations.

00:09:36.028 --> 00:09:38.789
I can only do what I can
deliver in this life.

00:09:38.789 --> 00:09:41.103
So I put that on my wrist
to remind me all times,

00:09:41.103 --> 00:09:42.567
that, like, trust in self.

00:09:42.567 --> 00:09:44.737
And you know, I will up.

00:09:44.737 --> 00:09:45.861
Doesn't mean I'm flawless.

00:09:45.861 --> 00:09:48.074
I made "Cop Out" and
"Jersey Girl," so you know,

00:09:48.074 --> 00:09:51.717
you can things up but you can
learn from that, you know.

00:09:51.717 --> 00:09:54.786
There's no such thing as absolute failure,

00:09:54.786 --> 00:09:57.879
except sitting there and not trying.

00:09:57.879 --> 00:10:01.350
That's absolute failure,
but even if you fail

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.994
at something, failure
is on a success train,

00:10:03.994 --> 00:10:07.197
because you learn how to
do it better the next time.

00:10:07.197 --> 00:10:09.963
As long as you try again, you're smarter

00:10:09.963 --> 00:10:11.406
than the average bear.

00:10:11.406 --> 00:10:14.164
Some people kind of try
something that falls apart,

00:10:14.164 --> 00:10:14.997
and they walk away.

00:10:14.997 --> 00:10:15.979
- Thank you, guys, so much for watching.

00:10:15.979 --> 00:10:17.808
I hope you enjoyed, I'd love to know,

00:10:17.808 --> 00:10:19.304
what did you think of the video?

00:10:19.304 --> 00:10:22.108
Which clip had the biggest
impact on you and why?

00:10:22.108 --> 00:10:23.289
Leave in the comments below,

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:24.834
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:10:24.834 --> 00:10:26.377
I also want to give a quick shout out

00:10:26.377 --> 00:10:28.920
to blackpearls87, thank you so much

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:31.197
for picking up a copy of
my book, "Your One Word,"

00:10:31.197 --> 00:10:32.570
and posting that picture on Twitter.

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:35.322
I hope you're enjoying it
and I appreciate the support.

00:10:35.322 --> 00:10:36.864
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:10:36.864 --> 00:10:39.022
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:10:39.022 --> 00:10:41.845
to believe in yourself, and
whatever your one word is.

00:10:41.845 --> 00:10:44.262
Much love, I'll see you soon.

